Experiment 1: Pointer to an Integer Variable
Aim: To declare a pointer, initialize it with the address of an integer variable, and display the value and address using both the variable and the pointer.
Program
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a = 10;
    int *p;

    p = &a;

    printf("Value of a = %d\n", a);
    printf("Address of a = %p\n", (void *)&a);

    printf("Value using pointer = %d\n", *p);
    printf("Address using pointer = %p\n", (void *)p);

    return 0;
}

Output
Value of a = 10
Address of a = 0x7ffe1234
Value using pointer = 10
Address using pointer = 0x7ffe1234

Note: The address shown above is an example. The actual address may differ when you run the program.

Explanation:
- int a = 10; declares an integer variable.
- int *p; declares a pointer to an integer.
- p = &a; stores the address of a in pointer p.
- *p accesses the value stored at that address.
- &a gives the address of variable a.
